@@ -0,0 +1,51 @@
+---
+id: dl-004-json-patch-diff-lcs-array-matching-by-deep-equality-no-move-detection-no-dedicated-error-type
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Patch diff: LCS array matching by deep equality, no move detection, no dedicated error type"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Clients want to send only what changed between two versions of a document instead of the whole new
+version. `createPatch(from, to)` computes an RFC 6902 JSON Patch that turns `from` into `to`, for use
+with the existing `applyPatch`. The requirement is that the patch "describes what changed, and nothing
+more": a small, local change must not produce a patch that replaces a whole enclosing object or array.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Objects are diffed member by member: a key missing from `to` becomes a `remove`, a key missing from
+  `from` becomes an `add`, and a key present in both recurses instead of being replaced wholesale. This
+  is what keeps a change nested deep inside a document scoped to that location, per the requirement.
+- Arrays are diffed as a longest common subsequence, matching elements by the same `deepEqual` that
+  `applyPatch`'s `test` operation already uses. Matched elements are left untouched; unmatched runs
+  become `add`/`remove` operations positioned against the array as it is transformed step by step. This
+  turns a single insertion or removal in the middle of an array into one operation instead of a
+  `replace` of every element shifted around it, which a naive index-by-index comparison would produce.
+- Matching is by exact deep equality only; there is no heuristic to pair up two unequal-but-similar
+  elements (e.g. the same record with one field changed) for a nested diff. Such a pair becomes a
+  `remove` of the old element and an `add` of the new one, in full. Detecting "this is the same record,
+  slightly changed" needs a similarity or identity heuristic (e.g. a key field) that this function has
+  no way to know; guessing one would be speculative, the same call dl-001 and dl-003 made about not
+  adding error-cause discriminants or array-shape leniency beyond what the inputs actually require.
+- There is no detection of `move` or `copy`: a value that reappears unchanged at a different location is
+  emitted as a `remove` at the old location and an `add` at the new one, never as a single `move`.
+  Detecting that would require matching across the whole document, not just within one object or array,
+  for a case the requirement (one small change producing a small patch) does not call for.
+- `createPatch` does not throw and no dedicated error type is exported. Every pair of JSON-compatible
+  values has a patch that turns one into the other (worst case, a single `replace` at the root), so
+  there is no failure case to report, the same reasoning dl-003 applied to `applyMergePatch`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Patches are minimal in operation count for additions, removals, and nested replacements, but not
+  globally minimal: a reordering of array elements, or an element changed enough that it no longer
+  matches its old self, costs a `remove`/`add` pair rather than a single `replace` or `move`. Callers
+  that need move-aware or similarity-aware diffs must layer that on top; this function only guarantees
+  "unchanged parts are left out," not "the absolute shortest possible patch."
+- Because array matching is by `deepEqual`, two structurally-equal-but-reference-distinct elements (e.g.
+  two separately-constructed objects with the same content) are treated as the same element and
+  produce no operation, consistent with how `applyPatch`'s `test` operation already treats equality
+  elsewhere in this module.
+- As with `applyMergePatch`, a caller cannot distinguish "nothing changed" from "malformed input" by
+  catching an exception, because the second case does not exist for this function.
